--TEST--
A markup literal's segments are written as the author typed them and every hole goes through the escape — `rule:core-classes/html-template`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The whole rule in one line: the segment is source, so its `&` stands; the
// hole is data, so its `&` and its angle brackets become references. This is
// the rule `<?= ?>` already follows, written as an expression.
var $name = "ada & <grace>";
echo html`<b>ada & co</b>: {$name}`, "\n";

// The hole grammar is the double-quoted string's, both halves of it: a bare
// `$name` interpolates in PHP's simple syntax, and it escapes exactly as the
// braced form does — the spelling of the hole says nothing about its trust.
echo html`<i>$name</i>`, "\n";

// A braced hole's body is a full expression closed by the matching brace, and
// what it computes is escaped whatever it was computed from.
var $tail = "<z>";
echo html`<p>{$name . $tail}</p>`, "\n";

// The result is a `Core\Html\Markup`, so it composes with the other trusted
// fragments rather than being escaped a second time on the way into them.
Core\Html\Markup $cell = html`<td>{$name}</td>`;
echo $cell + ("<td>plain</td>" as Core\Html\Markup), "\n";

// And escaping is not idempotent, which is why the carrier exists: text that
// already reads as a reference is escaped again, because that is what the
// author's data said.
var $already = "&amp;";
echo html`<p>{$already}</p>`, "\n";
--EXPECT--
<b>ada & co</b>: ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;
<i>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</i>
<p>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;&lt;z&gt;</p>
<td>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</td><td>plain</td>
<p>&amp;amp;</p>
